text:
@hoosieramerican In many cases, you can’t anymore. Some companies still issue physical stock certificates, but many no longer do.

The modern alternative is DRS — Direct Registration System. Your shares are registered directly in your own name on the company’s books through its transfer agent, but there’s no paper certificate.

You don't need a physical certificate to have legally recognized ownership of shares, though.

@BrutallyHonestFinance. In the 1980s when banks failed in the central states like Oklahoma.  They were paid on a sliding scale (it wasn’t FDIC, but the same concept).  This was left out of news reports 

It’s already been mentioned that the next bank failures will be handled with bail ins, again the FDIC doesn’t have the money 

When GMAC failed during the Great Recession it became Ally Bank.  The demand deposits were wiped out but all loans with them still had to be paid.  It happened to my father who lost 20k but still had to pay his 10k plus loan with them

@AwosAtis  You’re right that stocks/ETFs are different from cash. I used “money” in the title in the everyday sense of your brokerage wealth, but most of the video is specifically about invested assets.

As for valuations: if one brokerage failed, that wouldn’t necessarily change the value of the underlying companies or ETFs.

But if multiple major brokerages were to fail at the same time, then markets could definitely fall because of panic, liquidity problems, and broader financial stress. That’s market risk, though, which is separate from the custody risk I address in the video.

@BrutallyHonestFinance ok, thanks for more articulated answer

identity theft ... many people i think are unaware that the default settings expose them to this. the customer need to change their setting on what they allow

the customer should forbid remote transfers of money by brokerage out of their account other than their bank account
